it's great to welcome to the program  today Denise Hearn who is co-author of  the book the myth of capitalism  monopolies and the death of competition  which was named a best book of 2018 by  the Financial Times and is endorsed by  not one but two Nobel Prize winners  Denise really great to talk to you today  yeah thank you David for having me on so  this is a really interesting topic for a  few reasons one is that there's a very  sort of public conversation happening  about socialism and social democracy our  so-called democratic socialists actually  socialists or are they really Social  Democrats and how does that compare and  contrast with the system of capitalism  but a conversation that that's not  happening enough is do we even really  have the free market capitalist paradise  that many believe is the contrast to a  social democracy or democratic socialism  I believe that the answer is no and I  and I hope to get into some of the ways  in which that's the case but can you  kind of open up by talking about when we  talk about the United States as a  capitalist economy in what ways is it  but also in what ways is it really not  it's a great question because I think  when when we have these debates about  socialism versus capitalism people  haven't really defined terms and they  actually don't there's not agreement in  terms of what they actually mean and so  what they're arguing in favor for or  against we titled our book the myth of  capitalism because when we think about  the United States we think of it as the  bastion of free market capitalism  globally and as we did our research we  started to realize that actually the the  current a situation in the American  economy looks anything like free markets  it is controlled and dominated within  industries by you know handful of large  corporations across all industries  essentially so we're now in a position  where we have you know two companies  that control 75% of the beer that  Americans drink and for airlines that  dominate all air traffic and and you  know the list of industries with  dominant  endless and so what we discovered is  that you know it's not so much that  capitalism itself has a fundamental flaw  it's just that the current iteration of  what we're experiencing in the economy  is certainly not capitalism if you think  that capitalism as all dictionary  definitions to have must include  competition and so we've really moved  away from competitive marketplaces and  you know we think that that's incredibly  problematic for a number of reasons that  we discuss in the book yes so you've  pointed out the in some of the  industries in which we don't really have  the competition that so-called free  market capitalism claims to have or  claims that we should have we also have  any number of industries that are  heavily subsidized by the government and  when you hear the criticism that we  heard under the Obama administration  often from the right that the government  shouldn't pick winners and losers when  we heavily subsidize agribusiness when  we heavily subsidize the airline  industry when we talk about revitalizing  coal what we're talking about is  interrupting a free market that might  have coal die and saying we are going to  artificially prop that industry up so  there's the competition side and then  there's also and I'm curious whether how  you see this as related there's the  subsidy side where you see the  government interfering but in very  particular ways that are sort of within  the Overton Window of acceptability for  lack of a better term well and this is  where it's very interesting because you  recognize that the old the other old  debate about you know free markets  versus government interference is a  complete red herring because the  government has been bought by corporate  interests and we see that you know  there's just huge amounts of regulatory  capture where you have the executives  and the CEOs and the management teams of  large corporations going and on sitting  on the regulatory bodies that are  supposed to protect consumers from in  their best interests from you know  potentially nefarious interests on the  corporate side and so you see people  moving back and forth between the two  quite readily and this is why then you  get  you know potentially subsidies to  industries where otherwise the market  wouldn't be calling for them and you you  get into a situation where the companies  can then write the rules in their favor  they can actually influence the  regulator's we saw last year in big tech  all of the big the dominant firms there  spent records amount on lobbying and so  you see that there are you know  definitely very intentional spends and  activities that are trying to influence  regulators trying to influence you know  government decision making in their  favor there seems to be either I guess  we could call it a vicious cycle we  could call it a feedback loop where the  political system we have with the  influence peddling and kind of revolving  doors you're talking about of lobbyists  and elected officials and regulatory  capture that further allows the Oleg  appellee status quo to be perpetuated  and then the oligopoly status quo which  creates more and more powerful  corporations allows them to sort of  wield more influence over the political  system is has that always been the case  or is this acutely bad right now  well as you say it's totally self  reinforcing and in that kind of circular  way and I think that of course this is  you know in a way this has always  existed but I think it is particularly  bad at this point in history because one  of the main tools that's fallen away  from from being enforced to kind of  counteract this centralized power is  antitrust and we've seen a complete  drop-off in antitrust enforcement from  the DOJ and the FTC when the when the  original antitrust laws were first  introduced around the time of the Gilded  Age back you know at the turn of the  century there we had the big Trust's of  the day we had the Vanderbilts the  Rockefellers controlling entire  industries there too you know of  railroads and oil and people said that  they wanted the government to really  challenge and establish the kind of a  separation and definitely established  too  rules and laws that would help to limit  concentrated power in this way and I  think that you know we had a period of  great enforcement up through kind of the  the middle of the 1900s from the 30s  until the 80s and then in the 1980s  under President Reagan we had a  relaxation of merger guidelines as one  of the major contributing factors so  companies were allowed to merge really  without you know too much scrutiny and  all they had to do was say that they  would lower prices for consumers and as  long as they could sort of you know  claim that they would lower prices than  they were allowed to merge and so we've  seen you know decade after decade of  just merger wave after a merger wave  each one bigger than the last we saw you  know one of the largest merger years in  history under President Obama and then  to 2018 was another big one and so  what's that what that has done is then  it really has kind of precipitated this  consolidation within industries at the  same time the antitrust enforcement has  totally languished and so so really over  the last you know 30 to 40 years it has  become incredibly problematic and then  as you as you said at the beginning of  your question that has also helped to  precipitate this feedback loop between  kind of government and business interest  starting to more become more and more in  meshed is part of the problem that there  is not a huge difference in practice  between Democrats and Republicans on  this issue if we take a sort of zoomed  out view and I know that we have some  data points like Elizabeth Warren is now  talking about breaking up Amazon in  facebook although I think practically  speaking she doesn't do that or at least  not as presidential policy it would  depend on a big sort of justice system  initiative is it accurate to say that  even though the rhetoric is somewhat  different from Democrats and Republicans  that this is one of those issues that's  kind of within the general indifference  to the status quo that no one really  wants to get too deep with well I think  the historical legacy is certainly that  Democrats and Republicans have both been  equally complicit and allowing the  to happen you know as I mentioned the  mergers under Obama also there were more  visits to the White House ever than ever  in history by one company under Obama  and that was Google so but you can see  that you know yes essentially over the  last 40 years it doesn't really matter  who was in office neither neither party  took this as a priority I think now  we're in a situation where I am very  happy to see that this is entering kind  of the national conversation and you  know Elizabeth Warren has been  discussing these issues for the last few  years the break-up big tech I think was  a big splash and I'm very glad to see  that she's elevated the conversation in  this way you know I but I am hopeful  actually that this is strangely one of  the places that we could actually find  convergence as a country and that this  is a bipartisan issue because it affects  all Americans you know if you're a  worker you're essentially getting  screwed from this because you know  companies have the ability to well  they're just you you've had a real loss  of bargaining power and at the same time  that companies have you know increased  their their power relative to workers if  you're a consumer you're paying higher  prices for goods because when oligopoly  has become entrenched you they become  price makers and so you end up paying  you know higher prices for worse and  worse service because they're not  incentivized to serve you the airlines  are the best example of that and if  you're a supplier it's also equally  difficult because you're getting  squeezed out and so you know I think  these these questions aren't sort of  esoteric questions happening you know at  the at the level that you described in  turn in terms of you know regulatory and  legislative questions they really are  fundamental to kind of the fabric of our  democracy and the fabric of that the  type of economy that needs to work for  all Americans and so I think that's why  this is such an important conversation  and I'm really glad to see that that  some of the Democratic candidates are  taking it on I think we'll continue to  see Republican voices come to the  conversation as well and we've seen Josh  Haley of Missouri who is also a strong  Tec who's a Republican and so you know  there's there are increasingly more  conservative voices that are sort of  banging the drum as well but I'd love to  see even more it seems that there's the  the sort of like caricature conversation  which is free-market capitalism versus  quote socialism and then if we want to  actually have like an adult conversation  what we're really talking about is a  generally capitalist system that we  modify in different ways either we have  the sort of status quo we've had for  several decades which is we call it  capitalist but in reality we have  subsidies we have oligopolies we have  lack of enforcement when it comes to  antitrust or the social democracy model  that we're seeing in countries like  Sweden and Denmark and Norway where we  have a generally capitalist system but  we say we're going to prioritize social  welfare programs and pay for health  insurance and public college out of  taxation that seems to be the real  conversation but it's not really the one  that anybody's having yes I think I  don't agree more with you and I think  it's so silly in a way to say that we  want you know fully unfettered free  markets and the analogy I use is is that  you know if you showed up to a soccer  game and there were no pre-established  rules of engagement no rules to how you  score how many people are on a team you  know any there were no sort of structure  under which that game proceeds that  would be just be a horrible game to  watch nobody would want to watch it and  it would be subject to all kinds of you  know interference with things that  weren't helpful and I just think you  know the same is true with markets we  any marketplace is any type of exchange  is going to be subject to certain  expectations and certain rules and  structures that we've put in place to  help you know benefit the the and and to  help you know produce a conduit for that  exchange to take place so the the  discussion really isn't about kind of  free markets unfettered versus you know  big government socialism I think you're  exactly right that we need to have more  nuanced conversations about what kind  of capitalism what kind of you know what  kind of economy do we want to actually  be instituting and I'm actually Canadian  by birth and so you know we tend to skew  more sort of European as well in terms  of social democracy and I think that you  really see that you know these are  fundamental questions about when I think  about it you know I kind of think of  Maslow's hierarchy of needs and I think  what is an economy for an economy is for  much more than simply you know making  money what it is is that first you have  to have your basic needs met you know  then you move on to kind of the the  economy of belonging and creating  relationships and you know having  creating loving relationships finding a  partner yeah and then and then you get  to the self-actualization piece and  economies should be under girding the  human pursuit of of these these you know  beautiful journeys that we're all on and  I think you know that tends to get lost  in these conversations that are very  sort of there's a very divided in terms  of you know socialist virtues capitalist  and and as you said as well you know the  capitalism that we have in the United  States is incredibly different than the  capitalism in German Germany or Norway  and so I think there is so much more  room to have more nuanced conversations  along these lines about how we want to  be structuring you know all the various  pieces of this moving forward the book  is called the myth of capitalism  monopolies and the death of competition  we've been speaking with the books  co-author  Denis Hearn Denis thank you so much for  talking to us about this today thank you  very much

